SX-WSA1 / SX-WSA1R CONTROL PANEL -- the three edits that wire wsa1_cpanel up
===========================================================================

The device itself is complete and committed:
    src/mame/matsushita/wsa1_cpanel.h
    src/mame/matsushita/wsa1_cpanel.cpp

Everything it needs from the machine already exists EXCEPT one thing: a way for
a device to hand CPU 1 a received byte on serial channel 1 and raise INTRX1.
MAME's tmp95c061 has no serial engine at all -- sc1buf_r() returns 0, sc1buf_w()
only fakes "transmit complete" by setting INTES1 bit 7, and nothing anywhere
sets INTES1 bit 3.  It cannot be worked around from the driver, and here is the
proof rather than the assertion:

  * inte_w() (tmp95c061.cpp:1281-1290) PRESERVES bit 3 when a 1 is written to
    it -- `if (data & 0x08) data = (data & 0xf7) | (m_int_reg[offset] & 0x08)`.
    So a driver writing INTES1 through the CPU's own address space can clear
    the INTRX1 request flag but can never set it.
  * INTRX1 is not one of the TLCS900_* input lines (tlcs900.h:11-26), so
    set_input_line() cannot reach it either.
  * Routing the receive through INTTX1 instead does not work, even though the
    two vectors run byte-identical dispatchers (they differ in 1 byte of 40,
    a calr displacement, and both resolve to the same target): the firmware
    writes INTES1 = 0x05 for a receive turn, which puts INTTX1 at LEVEL 0, and
    tlcs900_check_irqs() only ever dispatches levels 1..6.

INT6 (the panel's attention line) needs no core work -- TLCS900_INT6 is a real
input line.  P8 bit 5 and PB bits 0 and 4 need no core work -- port8_read() and
portb_read() already exist.

