------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT TO EXPECT WHEN IT IS WIRED, and how to tell it worked
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. SC1_Vtable_0_Open (prom_b 0xF5A818) runs from the boot vtable walk at
   prom_a 0xF827DD and sends (0xDF,0xD2) (0xDF,0x1A) (0xDD,0x03) (0xDE,0x80).
   A tap on SC1BUF writes should show those eight bytes in that order.
2. SC1_Cmd_E0_ReadStatus sets bit 3 of RAM (0x2A85) iff the panel answered.
   Watch (0x002A85): 0x08 means the handshake completed.
3. RAM 0x20D0..0x20D7 is the firmware's LED want-buffer.  Once the link is up
   it should stop being all-zero, and every change should appear as a frame.
4. A button press should land in RAM 0x2B20 + 0x10 + segment (the panel's own
   shadow), then as a 3-byte record in the inbound queue at 0x2B4A.., then as
   a {group, data, changed} triple in the event array at 0x2000 with the count
   at (0x219A).
5. The negative control: hold segment 8 bits 0 and 1 at power-on and the boot
   block must take the FACTORY CLEAR path at prom_a 0xF828E9 -- it zeroes RAM,
   writes 0x5AA5 at 0x7FCA and jumps back to RESET.  Nothing else in this
   machine produces that signature, so seeing it proves the whole chain.
